Name: cppgc
Short Name: cppgc
URL: https://chromium.googlesource.com/v8/v8.git
Version: N/A
Revision: 913d2ab381f8843bb1f918f1a7b40c0aae5ba8f0
License: BSD-3-Clause
License File: v8/LICENSE
Security Critical: yes
Shipped: yes

Description:
cppgc is the garbage collector that backs Blink's Oilpan. It lives inside the
V8 repository, at include/cppgc and src/heap/cppgc-internal, but it is a
self-contained library that V8's own build can compile standalone.

Why it is vendored here:

This tree deletes V8. That is straightforward for the JavaScript engine, and
impossible for the collector, because blink::GarbageCollected<T> is a direct
alias for cppgc::GarbageCollected<T> --

    // third_party/blink/renderer/platform/heap/garbage_collected.h
    template <typename T>
    using GarbageCollected = cppgc::GarbageCollected<T>;

-- with no abstraction layer in between, and every DOM node, CSS rule and layout
object in Blink derives from it. Removing cppgc would mean replacing Blink's
object model, not removing a dependency.

Splitting the two is cheap. cppgc's entire external surface is two headers,
v8config.h and v8-platform.h (which pulls v8-source-location.h), plus abseil,
which Chromium already has. The vendored set is 350 files and 2.2 MiB against
V8's 147 MiB, and none of it can reach the parser, the interpreter, TurboFan,
the snapshot or the bindings.

Local modifications:

None to the source. The files are copied verbatim by
//tools/shot/vendor_cppgc.py, which also generates sources.gni. Implementations
for platforms other than Windows are not copied, and neither are tests; V8
selects those with GN conditionals we do not reproduce.

The build differs from V8's in one way that matters, recorded in BUILD.gn:
CPPGC_IS_STANDALONE is on, and caged heap, young generation and pointer
compression are off.

To re-vendor at a different revision:

  python3 tools/shot/vendor_cppgc.py <v8-checkout> --revision <sha>
